## The short version

Bring a **real draft** of your <course-link type="assignment" id="cp3">Written Proposal (CP3)</course-link> to the peer-review session, and turn it in two ways: **upload it here**, and **bring two printed copies** to be marked up on paper in class. Scans or photos of both marked-up copies go in with your final CP3 submission.

---

## Why this assignment exists

A draft that only you have read is not a draft — it's a first attempt you're still inside of. The value of a review is that someone who owes you nothing tells you where they stopped believing you, and the only way to get that is to hand them something complete enough to react to.

This is how technical writing works everywhere: an RFC goes out before it's final, a design doc gets circulated for comment, a pull request is opened while it can still change. The people who benefit most are the ones who send the *whole* thing early and unpolished rather than four beautiful sections and an apology. A rough full draft earns a far better review than a polished fragment, because a reviewer can only test an argument they can see the shape of.

*In this course, that principle has teeth:* the CP3 rubric scores **Argument and revision**, which asks what changed between your draft and your final. **With no draft on file, that criterion is capped at Developing** — not as a penalty, but because there is nothing to measure revision against.

---

## What to produce

A draft of your proposal covering, at minimum:

1. **Problem and users** — who has this problem and what evidence says so
2. **Solution** — what you propose to build
3. **Non-goals** — what you are deliberately scoping out
4. **Technical approach** — how you would build it
5. **Risks** — your three biggest, with mitigations

Sections beyond these are welcome. Sections missing from these will simply go unreviewed, which is the loss you are trying to avoid.

